Insightful's answer on the multiple numbers in dataframes is below:

Dear Yannis Tzamouranis, Jonathan Dakin, and Lorenzo Vilizzi,

A problem has been identified in S-PLUS 2000, 6.0 and 6.1 as the result of
a recent Microsoft Windows Hotfix / update patch.  The specific symptoms
have only been noted in the S-PLUS's data grid editor.  The easiest way to
reproduce the problem is as follows:

1)  Create a new data sheet (File -> New -> Data Set)
2)  Start typing in one of the cells.  You should quickly note, for
example, that typing "2" produces "222", etc.

This issue does not affect the core statistical functionality of S-PLUS,
but only affects the data editor in the Windows GUI as noted above.

If you are using Windows 2000, this issue is the result of Microsoft
Security Patch KB824141.
Additional information from Microsoft is available at the following pages:
http://support.microsoft.com/?kbid=831739
http://www.microsoft.com/technet/security/bulletin/MS03-045.asp

If you are using Windows XP, the problem is also the result of a security
patch but the exact patch number is not available.  The problem has been
seen in Windows XP SP 2.

This problem is fixed in S-PLUS 6.2 for Windows.

Users who do not use the Data Grid Editor in S-PLUS will be unaffected by
this problem.  Users who wish to resolve the problem in S-PLUS 2000, 6.0 or
S-PLUS 6.1 should temporarily remove the offending Microsoft patch.  To
remove the patch, go to Control Panel -> Add/Remove Programs and remove the
item named "Windows 2000 Hotfix - KB824141."  It will be necessary to
restart your computer after the removal is complete.

One workaround to the problem, for those who are unable to remove the patch
for various reasons, is to type a leading zero (0) at the beginning of any
number entered.  For non-numeric strings, it should be sufficient to cut
and paste into the window any strings you wish to enter.

We apologize for any inconvenience.

