Rich,

Features of "random"?
I'm afraid I don't understand what you mean by this.

My concern is that I am sacrificing the inferential strength of the
experiment.

The area harvested was chosen randomly from selected stands in the
province (Ontario) with a predetermined species composition.

The cut blocks (blk) were arranged in an orderly manner with harvest
treatments (trt) randomly assigned to each.
The plots (plt) within each block were randomly assigned combinations
of the sub-level treatment groupings.
Individual trees were measured within this split-plot arrangement and
a few responses were measured within a further division of this.

I believe that I mistakenly inferred that the orderly arrangement
of the plots meant that the replicative factors were fixed.
I now believe that I should have set the blk & rep factors as random.

Now, if I understand this whole issue correctly, by using fixed factors
for replication I have generated artificially narrow confidence limits.
Is this so? What are the other concerns?

I very, very much appreciate your comments.

Thank you, DaveT.

