From spencer.graves at pdf.com  Fri Dec  3 06:42:48 2004
From: spencer.graves at pdf.com (Spencer Graves)
Date: Thu, 02 Dec 2004 22:42:48 -0800
Subject: Re: Random or fixed factors?
In-Reply-To: <3B1DF09C9A08D3118D310008C7913C4512C4BB4B@rlc00aex006.mnr.gov.on.ca>
References: <3B1DF09C9A08D3118D310008C7913C4512C4BB4B@rlc00aex006.mnr.gov.on.ca>
Message-ID: <41B00AE8.8010007@pdf.com>

     For me, "Random Effects for Dummies" simplifies to whether the 
effects can plausibly be considered as random samples from a larger 
population.  If the answer is "yes", then it's a random effect.  In 
particular, blocks and plots would only be fixed effects if the 
following two conditions held: 

     (1) We were only interested in knowing whether the specific blocks 
and plots studied were different and if so how much better or worse each 
one was relative to the others. 

     (2) The specific blocks and plots used could NOT plausible be 
considered representative of some other population with its own 
variability. 

     Conversely, I would specify something as a random effect if the 
specific units (blocks and plots) in the study could plausibly be be 
considered a random selection from some population. 

     (a) Often, I'm interested in estimating the contribution to (i.e., 
component of) variance from that particular source. 

     (b) Occasionally, I'm interested in how different particular units 
are from others.  In the latter case I would like to use the shrinkage 
provide by the "Stein-type estimator" pulling the different units toward 
their mean as provided naturally by the objective Bayesian perspective 
represented by the standard variance components model. 

     Bottom line:  I would nearly always treat blocks and plots as 
random effects. 

     If I have misunderstood or misrepresented something, I hope that 
one of the real experts on this list will attempt to enlighten me. 

     hope this helps. 
     spencer graves

