Subject: Re: Random or fixed factors?
In-Reply-To: <41B00AE8.8010007@pdf.com>
References: <3B1DF09C9A08D3118D310008C7913C4512C4BB4B@rlc00aex006.mnr.gov.on.ca> <41B00AE8.8010007@pdf.com>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.61.0412030735420.28412@gannet.stats>

What you have not considered is the potential benefit of conditional 
inference.

Although the blocks in a randomized block design should according to your 
conditions be treated as random (and they are indeed random effects), 
conventional wisdom conditions on the block arrangement actually used, in 
just the same way that regression conditions on the x values actually 
obtained.

There are two steps involved here: are the effects *by design* fixed or 
random, and should the appropriate conditional inference include 
conditioning on the particular samples obtained.

I won't go into here the rationale for conditional inference, just remark 
that people such as Fisher and Cox have thought deeply about it but it 
seems not to make many modern textbooks.

On Thu, 2 Dec 2004, Spencer Graves wrote:

>     For me, "Random Effects for Dummies" simplifies to whether the effects
> can plausibly be considered as random samples from a larger population.  If
> the answer is "yes", then it's a random effect.  In particular, blocks and
> plots would only be fixed effects if the following two conditions held:
>     (1) We were only interested in knowing whether the specific blocks and
> plots studied were different and if so how much better or worse each one was
> relative to the others.
>     (2) The specific blocks and plots used could NOT plausible be considered
> representative of some other population with its own variability.
>     Conversely, I would specify something as a random effect if the specific
> units (blocks and plots) in the study could plausibly be be considered a
> random selection from some population.
>     (a) Often, I'm interested in estimating the contribution to (i.e.,
> component of) variance from that particular source.
>     (b) Occasionally, I'm interested in how different particular units are
> from others.  In the latter case I would like to use the shrinkage provide by
> the "Stein-type estimator" pulling the different units toward their mean as
> provided naturally by the objective Bayesian perspective represented by the
> standard variance components model.

Neither of those analogies is accurate, and additional shrinkage can be 
beneficial.

>     Bottom line:  I would nearly always treat blocks and plots as random
> effects.
>     If I have misunderstood or misrepresented something, I hope that one of
> the real experts on this list will attempt to enlighten me.
>     hope this helps.      spencer graves
