From david.thompson at mnr.gov.on.ca  Fri Dec  3 15:59:14 2004
From: david.thompson at mnr.gov.on.ca
Date: Fri, 03 Dec 2004 10:59:14 -0500
Subject: Re: contrast reference level
Message-ID: <3B1DF09C9A08D3118D310008C7913C4512C4BB65@rlc00aex006.mnr.gov.on.ca>

-----Original Message-----
From: array chip [mailto:arrayprofile at yahoo.com] 
Sent: December 2, 2004 6:29 PM
To: s-news at lists.biostat.wustl.edu
Subject: contrast reference level

suppose I have a factor with 4 levels:
'a','b','c','d'. I would like to do analysis of
variance using aov() with the factor as independent
variable. How can I specify the level "b" as the
reference level just like the level "a" would be the
reference level if using contr.treatment as the
contrast? In R, relevel() can do this, is there a
similar uitlity in S-Plus?

Thanks

-----Response-----
I don't know of a single function for this but I use
the following application of a matrix to set contrasts:

> test <- data.frame(c(letters[1:4]))
> test
  X1 
1  a
2  b
3  c
4  d

(Helmert contrasts are default for factors on my system)
> contrasts(test$X1)
  [,1] [,2] [,3] 
a   -1   -1   -1
b    1   -1   -1
c    0    2   -1
d    0    0    3

> contrasts(test$X1) <- matrix(c(-1,1,0,0,0,1,-1,0,0,1,0,-1),4,3)

> contrasts(test$X1)
  [,1] [,2] [,3] 
a   -1    0    0
b    1    1    1
c    0   -1    0
d    0    0   -1

Check for independence:
        Sum of each column must equal zero.

Check for orthogonality:
        Sum each row, then sum of this additional column must equal zero.

View these single-df planned comparisons:
        testaov <- aov(formula, data=test, ...)
        summary(testaov, split = list(X1 = list(b vs a = 1, b vs c = 2, b vs
d = 3)))

The outer list names all factors of concern, the inner list(s) label each
level of each factor partitioned.

Any sensible set (up to X1 df) of planned comparisons are possible this way.

DaveT.
