From r.verwer at nih.knaw.nl  Fri Dec  3 15:11:44 2004
From: r.verwer at nih.knaw.nl (Ronald WH Verwer)
Date: Fri, 03 Dec 2004 16:11:44 +0100
Subject: parameter constraints in nlme?
Message-ID: <41B09040.24563.1860314@localhost>

Dear All,

I use S+ version 6.2 on Windows XP. My colleague Eus van
Someren and I try to analyze circadian hormone levels in patients
using nlme. We have problems with convergence and wondered
whether it is possible and helpful to impose constraints on the
parameters in nlme. We know that nlregb can be used in
constrained regression, but we would like to retain the multilevel
character of the data. 

Any hints or comments are appreciated.

Detailed description of the problem ;

We meet the following problem in fitting nonlinear periodic
functions, that can moreover not be linearized, to hierarchically
structured data. Our major problem is how to set constraints to
the parameters that have to be estimated within a nlme regression
analysis.

The dataset consists of hormone levels measured i times during
day j for subject k. Typically, between between 5 and 12 samples
(i) are taken during one day; between 1 and 9 days (j) are
available; and about 150 subjects (k) are included in the study.

The nonlinear periodic functions we want to fit to the data are:

(1) the baseline cosine function with period 2*pi = 24 hours for
one hormone, as in attached figure 1. (Ruf, 1996)

Y = B + (H / ( 2 * (1-C))) * (COS(x - F) – C + |COS(x - F) - C| )

where Y is the hormone level on time x (in radians), B is the
baseline, H is the heigth, F is the acrophase and C defines the
part of the cosine above baseline.

(2) the skewed cosine function with period 2*pi = 24 hours for
another hormone, as in attached figure 2
(Batschelet, 1981):

Y = M + A * COS(F + x + V * COS(F + x))

where Y is the hormone level on time x (in radians), M is the
mesor, A is the amplitude, F is the acrophase and V defines the
skewedness.

Both functions require to put constraints on the values that the
parameters can adopt, i.e.:

Most importantly:

C should be limited to the range {-1,1}

V should be limited to the range {-0.5, 0.5} radians

In addition, for convenience of interpretation, as to match
possible physical realizations:

B and M above zero

F between zero and 2*pi

As stated, our major problem is thus how to set constraints to the
parameters that have to be estimated within a nlme regression
analysis..

References

Batschelet E (1981): Circular Statistics
in Biology, Academic
Press, London.

Ruf T (1996) The baseline cosinus function:
a periodic
regression model for biological rhythms. Biol Rhythm Res
27:153-165.

Thanks in advance,

Ronald

