> jackknifed model coefficients  e.g.
> jackknife(data,coef(glm(response~parameter,binomial), or even simple
> means e.g. jackknife(data, mean(parameter)), the values always seem to
> be the same as the mean of the real data values.
>
> Given this:
> - how does one obtain the predicted values from S-Plus jackknifing?
> - am I making a simle mistake so that my jackknifed values are similar
> to the real values?

>From the help page

   statistic
          statistic to be jackknifed. It may be a function which accepts
          data as the first argument and returns a vector or matrix, or a
          call referring to the data which evaluates to a vector or
          matrix. ...

I think therefore you need coef(glm(response~parameter,binomial, 
data=data), so that your statistic is evaluated at the jackknife 
datasets.

I am afraid I don't know what you mean by

> predicted jackknifed values for each sample point.

and do wonder if you are really looking for leave-one-out cross-validation 
(which is often called, incorrectly, `jackknifing').  What jackknifing is 
doing here is creating new estimates of the coefficients by LOO CV and 
combining them.

