At Friday 12:47 AM 12/3/2004, Prof Brian Ripley wrote:
> What you have not considered is the potential benefit of conditional
> inference.
>
>
>
> Although the blocks in a randomized block design should according to your
> conditions be treated as random (and they are indeed random effects),
> conventional wisdom conditions on the block arrangement actually used, in
> just the same way that regression conditions on the x values actually obtained.
>
>
>
> There are two steps involved here: are the effects *by design* fixed or
> random, and should the appropriate conditional inference include
> conditioning on the particular samples obtained.
>
>
>
> I won't go into here the rationale for conditional inference, just remark
> that people such as Fisher and Cox have thought deeply about it but it
> seems not to make many modern textbooks.
>
>
>
> On Thu, 2 Dec 2004, Spencer Graves wrote:
>
>
>
> >     For me, "Random Effects for Dummies" simplifies to whether the
> > effects can plausibly be considered as random samples from a larger
> > population.  If the answer is "yes", then it's a random effect.  In
> > particular, blocks and plots would only be fixed effects if the
> > following two conditions held:     (1) We were only interested in
> > knowing whether the specific blocks and plots studied were different and
> > if so how much better or worse each one was relative to the
> > others.     (2) The specific blocks and plots used could NOT plausible
> > be considered representative of some other population with its own
> > variability.     Conversely, I would specify something as a random
> > effect if the specific units (blocks and plots) in the study could
> > plausibly be be considered a random selection from some
> > population.     (a) Often, I'm interested in estimating the contribution
> > to (i.e., component of) variance from that particular source.     (b)
> > Occasionally, I'm interested in how different particular units are from
> > others.  In the latter case I would like to use the shrinkage provide by
> > the "Stein-type estimator" pulling the different units toward their mean
> > as provided naturally by the objective Bayesian perspective represented
> > by the standard variance components model.
>
> Neither of those analogies is accurate, and additional shrinkage can be
> beneficial.

Can lme() be used to get additional shrinkage? (other than by adding 
additional rows to the design matrix, as is described in Gelman, Gelman, 
Stern & Rubin, for example, though I don't know enough about lme() to know 
if it's feasible to do this neatly with lme()).  I skimmed through Pinhiero 
& Bates, but was unable to glean much about how one might be able to 
achieve more control over shrinkage using the parameters of lme().  Any 
pointers to discussions of how to do this with lme() would be greatly 
appreciated.

thanks,

-- Tony Plate

