     I believe the reason Ripley & Liaw suggested reparameterization is 
because the equality constraint makes it essentially a 2-dimensional 
minimization problem;  the third variable is (almost) determined from 
the other two.  Transforming to spherical coordinates effectively 
enforces the constraint.  In spherical coordinates, the problem will 
have multiple, equivalent solutions.  Almost any algorithm could have 
problems with this if the minimum occurred on a boundary.  If it were my 
problem, I think I would transform to spherical coordinates, x = 
cos(theta)sin(phi), y=sin(theta)sin(phi), z=cos(phi) 
[http://mathworld.wolfram.com/SphericalCoordinates.html], then make a 
contour plot of f(x,y,z) over 0<=theta<=2*pi, 0<=phi<=pi.  Examining 
this plot (or just finding the minimum over this grid) should provide 
good starting values, from which nlminb [or optim in library(MASS)] 
should work fine without constraints. 

     If there are other reasons for suggesting reparamaterization in 
this case, I hope someone will add that to this thread. 

     hope this helps. 
     spencer graves

