From LUCKE at uthscsa.edu  Mon Dec 13 16:49:35 2004
From: LUCKE at uthscsa.edu (Lucke, Joseph F)
Date: Mon, 13 Dec 2004 10:49:35 -0600
Subject: Re: multinomial regression
Message-ID: <C4A57662D47C7B44B781D39E4C8F06940E33A2@SAIGA.win.uthscsa.edu>

Natalie

You do not have a multinomial dependent variable as mentioned in Dr.
Ripley's second comment. You have 12 binomial dependent variables. The
12 categories are not mutually exclusive as is required for a
multinomial random variable.

Consider y as 3 (rather than 12) binomial variables with 0/1 outcomes. 

>y =
as.matrix(cbind(rbinom(100,1,.3),rbinom(100,1,.5),rbinom(100,1,.7))))

There are no columns with all zero entries

> sum(y[,1])
[1] 34
> sum(y[,2])
[1] 49
> sum(y[,3])
[1] 75  

But y is not multinomial as

> apply(y,1,sum)
  [1] 1 2 1 1 2 2 0 2 2 2 1 2 3 1 2 1 1 1 0 2 2 3 2 3 2 2 2 1 1 0 1 3 1
2 3 2 2 2 3 2 2 2 2
 [44] 1 0 0 2 2 1 3 3 1 1 3 1 2 1 2 0 2 1 1 0 2 1 2 1 2 0 2 1 1 2 2 0 3
2 1 2 1 2 2 1 2 1 2
 [87] 2 1 3 1 2 1 1 2 2 0 3 0 1 3

For y to be multinomial, each row should sum to 1.

But you have zeros in some cases, as your 3 binomials do not generate
mutually exclusive events.
Therefore you get

> multinom(y~1)
Problem in multinom(y ~ 1): some case has no observations 
Use traceback() to see the call stack

