From timh at insightful.com  Mon Dec 13 17:45:59 2004
From: timh at insightful.com (Tim Hesterberg)
Date: Mon, 13 Dec 2004 09:45:59 -0800
Subject: Re: Jackknifing
In-Reply-To: <41B40903.F08DC2F7@adelaide.edu.au>
References: <41B40903.F08DC2F7@adelaide.edu.au>
Message-ID: <SE2KEXCH0142s9Bcmc100002a08@se2kexch01.insightful.com>

>Dear S-Plus users,
>
>I am attempting to jackknife binomial GLM models (with both continuous
>and categorical parameters) in an attempt to produce ROC curves for
>model validation. However, I am unsure how to save / determine the
>predicted jackknifed values for each sample point.
>
>Also, although I am at the stage of being able to determine the
>jackknifed model coefficients  e.g.
>       jackknife(data, coef(glm(response ~ parameter, binomial)))
>, or even simple
>means e.g. jackknife(data, mean(parameter)), the values always seem to
>be the same as the mean of the real data values.

You need a connection between "data" and the variables used when
calling glm.  Try:

        jackknife(data, coef(glm(response ~ parameter, binomial, data = data)))

Otherwise, the resampled `data' won't be used.

Note that with the resample library (see bottom), 
you can use a simpler syntax:

        fit = glm(skips ~ ., family = poisson, data = solder.balance)
        jackknife(fit, coef)

>Given this:
>- how does one obtain the predicted values from S-Plus jackknifing?

I assume you mean the statistic computed for each of the n jackknife
data sets.  That is the "replicates" component of the jackknife object.

