Message-ID: <6.1.0.6.2.20041213104431.0617db90@mailhost.blackmesacapital.com>

Calculating age in years by dividing age in days by 365.25 might be OK for 
medical research, but if you used it in a cake shop for calculating the 
number of candles you might have some unhappy customers :-)

The best way I know to calculate the number of calendar years that have 
passed (AFAIK, the conventional definition of 'age in years') is to use the 
timeRelative class.  However, timeRelative can only be used to calculate 
one date from another -- I don't know of any way of getting a timeRelative 
object from two dates.  So, below is a function that attempts to compute 
the number of calendar years that have passed.  Unfortunately, timeRelative 
is not vectorized, so it needs a loop.

# 'ans' is the manually calculated number of calendar years that

# 'Event' is past 'DOB'

# This data contains some tricky test cases.

Event                     DOB   ans

07/11/2004          01/22/1965   39

06/12/2004          02/12/1974   30

08/11/2004          08/01/1959   45

02/28/2005          02/28/2004    1

02/28/2004          02/28/2003    1

02/27/2005          02/28/2004    0

02/27/2004          02/28/2003    0

03/01/2005          02/28/2004    1

02/29/2004          02/28/2003    1

03/01/2005          03/01/2004    1

03/01/2004          03/01/2003    1

02/29/2004          03/01/2003    0

03/01/2005          03/01/2004    1

02/28/2004          03/01/2003    0

> x <- read.table(clipboardConnection(), header=T, row.names=NULL)

> x$Event <- smart.timeDate(x$Event)

> x$DOB <- smart.timeDate(x$DOB)

> x$days <- as.numeric(x$Event-x$DOB)

> x$avgyears <- as.numeric(x$Event-x$DOB)/365.25

> # none of round(avgyears), floor(avgyears) or ceiling(avgyears) give the 
correct answer!

> x$ay.rnd <- round(x$avgyears)

> x$ay.flr <- floor(x$avgyears)

> x$ay.ceil <- ceiling(x$avgyears)

> cal.years <- function(from, to) {

+     caly <- numeric(length(from))

+     for (i in seq(along=from)) {

+         ay.rnd <- round(as.numeric(to[i] - from[i]) / 365.25)

+         target <- from[i]+timeRelative(by="years", k.by=ay.rnd)

+         targetp1 <- from[i]+timeRelative(by="years", k.by=ay.rnd+1)

+         caly[i] <- if (to[i]<target) ay.rnd-1 else if (to[i]>=targetp1) 
ay.rnd+1 else ay.rnd

+     }

+     return(caly)

+ }

>

> cbind(x, caly=cal.years(x$DOB, x$Event))

        Event        DOB ans  days   avgyears ay.rnd ay.flr ay.ceil caly

 1 2004/07/11 1965/01/22  39 14415 39.4661191     39     39      40   39

 2 2004/06/12 1974/02/12  30 11078 30.3299110     30     30      31   30

 3 2004/08/11 1959/08/01  45 16447 45.0294319     45     45      46   45

 4 2005/02/28 2004/02/28   1   366  1.0020534      1      1       2    1

 5 2004/02/28 2003/02/28   1   365  0.9993155      1      0       1    1

 6 2005/02/27 2004/02/28   0   365  0.9993155      1      0       1    0

 7 2004/02/27 2003/02/28   0   364  0.9965777      1      0       1    0

 8 2005/03/01 2004/02/28   1   367  1.0047912      1      1       2    1

 9 2004/02/29 2003/02/28   1   366  1.0020534      1      1       2    1

10 2005/03/01 2004/03/01   1   365  0.9993155      1      0       1    1

11 2004/03/01 2003/03/01   1   366  1.0020534      1      1       2    1

12 2004/02/29 2003/03/01   0   365  0.9993155      1      0       1    0

13 2005/03/01 2004/03/01   1   365  0.9993155      1      0       1    1

14 2004/02/28 2003/03/01   0   364  0.9965777      1      0       1    0

>

> # Note that 'caly' is the only column that is the same as 'ans'

Are there any easier ways to compute number of years that have passed 
between two dates?

