While mean() works on a dataframe, the problem is that stdev() does 
not.  To see exactly what your subfunction is getting passed, you can use 
browser() as follows:

> x <- data.frame(cbind(x1=rep(1:2,5), x2=runif(10)))
> by(x$x2,x$x1,function(xx) browser())
Called from: FUN(x[i,  , drop = F])
b(FUN)> xx
          x
1 0.6883236
3 0.1291990
5 0.1422239
7 0.3989520
9 0.7646130
b(FUN)> mean(x)
[1] 1.049702
b(FUN)> class(x)
[1] "data.frame"
b(FUN)> stdev(x)
Problem in as.double: Cannot coerce mode list to double: list(c(1., 2., 1.,
        2., 1., 2., 1., 2., 1., 2.), c(0.688323602546006,       0.7....
b(FUN)> stdev(x[[1]])
[1] 0.5270463
b(FUN)> stop
Problem: Asked from browser to stop current task
>

Now that we know that the subfunction here is getting a dataframe with one 
column, use an anonymous function to call stdev() appropriately:

> by(x$x2,x$x1,function(xx) stdev(xx[[1]]))
